insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('a1-q-800','aplus1',5,'5.1','ordering',2,'A workstation locks up at random since a memory upgrade. Place the technician''s actions in the order prescribed by the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology.','["Remove the newly added memory module and confirm the workstation runs cleanly", "Record the faulty module part number, the corrective action, and the result in the ticket", "Ask the user what changed recently and back up their data before opening the case", "Have the user run their normal workload for a full day and confirm no further lockups", "Suspect that the memory module added yesterday is faulty", "Order a matched replacement module, install it, and reassemble the machine"]'::jsonb,'[2, 4, 0, 5, 3, 1]'::jsonb,'Identify the problem (question the user about changes and back up before touching anything), establish a theory (the new module), test the theory (pull it and observe), establish a plan and implement it (source and fit a matched replacement), verify full functionality (a real day of the user''s workload, not just a POST), then document. The common error is putting the backup after the repair - the methodology places it inside step 1, before any change is made.'),
('a1-q-801','aplus1',5,'5.1','ordering',3,'A technician''s first theory about a blank display is disproved during testing. Place the following actions in the order the CompTIA methodology requires.','["Test the second theory by connecting the monitor to the motherboard''s onboard video output", "Establish a new theory: the discrete graphics card has failed", "Test the first theory by fitting a known-good video cable, which does not restore the picture", "Establish a first theory: the video cable is faulty", "Establish a plan of action, obtain an approved replacement card, and install it"]'::jsonb,'[3, 2, 1, 0, 4]'::jsonb,'Step 3 states that if the theory is not confirmed, you re-establish a new theory (or escalate) - you do not advance to a plan of action on a theory you failed to prove. So the sequence loops back through steps 2 and 3 a second time before implementation begins. Jumping straight from the failed cable test to ordering a graphics card would mean spending money on an untested guess.'),
('a1-q-802','aplus1',5,'5.1','ordering',2,'A user reports that their laptop shuts down during video calls. Place the technician''s actions in methodology order.','["Theorize that the intake vents are clogged and the CPU is overheating", "Note in the ticket that the vents were blocked, that they were cleared, and that the shutdowns stopped", "Run a 30-minute video call while monitoring CPU temperature to confirm the laptop stays up", "Ask the user when the shutdowns started, whether they happen on battery or AC, and what else is running", "Blow out the vents and heat sink with compressed air and reassemble the laptop", "Load the CPU with a stress test and watch the temperature climb to the shutdown threshold"]'::jsonb,'[3, 0, 5, 4, 2, 1]'::jsonb,'Gather symptoms and context first, form the cheapest testable theory, prove it by reproducing the shutdown under controlled load, then clean and reassemble, then verify with the workload that originally failed, then document. Cleaning the vents before the stress test would feel efficient but destroys the evidence - you would never know whether heat was actually the cause.'),
('a1-q-803','aplus1',5,'5.1','mcq',3,'A technician has confirmed that a failing NIC is causing intermittent outages on a production file server and has a compatible spare on the bench. Company policy requires a change ticket and an approved maintenance window for any server hardware change. What should the technician do NEXT?','["Submit the change request and schedule the swap for the approved maintenance window", "Install the spare NIC immediately, since the root cause has already been confirmed", "Document the findings and close the ticket as resolved", "Re-test the theory with a second known-good NIC before taking any other action"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'The methodology explicitly requires considering corporate policies, procedures, and impact before implementing changes - a confirmed diagnosis authorizes a plan of action, not an unscheduled production outage. Option 2 is the tempting one because the cause is proven and the part is in hand, but taking a file server down outside a window is an out-of-process change. Documentation (option 3) belongs after the fix is implemented and verified, and re-testing a confirmed theory (option 4) adds nothing.'),
('a1-q-804','aplus1',5,'5.1','mcq',2,'Which action does the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology place inside the "identify the problem" step, before any repair is attempted?','["Performing a backup of the user''s data, if applicable, before making changes", "Referring to the vendor''s instructions for guidance", "Implementing preventive measures so the issue does not recur", "Conducting external or internal research based on the symptoms"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Step 1 bundles three things: gather information from the user, identify what the user changed, and if applicable perform backups before making changes. Research based on symptoms (option 4) is the most tempting distractor because it feels like early information gathering, but CompTIA lists it under step 2, establishing a theory. Vendor instructions belong to step 4, and preventive measures to step 5.'),
('a1-q-805','aplus1',5,'5.1','mcq',3,'A technician has tested two theories about a server RAID controller fault; neither was confirmed, and the technician has exhausted their knowledge of that controller family. What does the methodology direct them to do NEXT?','["Establish a new theory of probable cause, or escalate the issue", "Establish a plan of action based on whichever failed theory seemed most likely", "Verify full system functionality and implement preventive measures", "Document the findings so far and close the ticket as unresolved"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Step 3 spells out both branches: if the theory is confirmed, determine the next steps to resolve; if it is not confirmed, re-establish a new theory or escalate. Option 2 is the classic trap - acting on an unproven theory means swapping controller hardware on a guess, which can introduce a second fault and destroy the evidence. Closing the ticket (option 4) abandons the user, and verification (option 3) presupposes a fix that has not happened.'),
('a1-q-806','aplus1',5,'5.1','mcq',2,'After replacing a failed power supply, the technician presses the power button and the PC boots to the Windows sign-in screen. The user is standing beside the desk. What is the BEST next action?','["Have the user sign in and exercise the applications and peripherals that were failing", "Document the findings, actions, and outcomes in the ticket", "Establish a preventive maintenance schedule for every PC in the office", "Return the failed power supply to the vendor for RMA"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Step 5 requires verifying FULL system functionality, and reaching a sign-in screen is not that - USB devices, the display at the correct resolution, network shares, and the user''s actual applications all draw on the new PSU and may still fail. Documentation (option 2) is genuinely the next step in the list, which is exactly why it is tempting, but it comes after verification, not instead of it.'),
('a1-q-807','aplus1',5,'5.1','mcq',1,'A user says their desktop "will not turn on at all." The technician''s first theory is that the motherboard has failed. What flaw does the CompTIA methodology identify in this approach?','["It skips the obvious - the mains lead, wall outlet, PSU rocker switch, and power-button header should be questioned first", "A theory may only be established after a plan of action has been written", "A technician may not establish any theory until the ticket has been escalated", "Motherboard faults may only be theorized about after consulting vendor documentation"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Step 2 carries the explicit instruction "question the obvious." The simplest causes are the most common and cost seconds to rule out, whereas theorizing a board failure first leads to an expensive, slow, and probably unnecessary swap. Options 2 and 3 invert the methodology - a plan of action follows a confirmed theory, and escalation is what you do when theories fail, not a prerequisite for forming one.'),
('a1-q-808','aplus1',5,'5.1','multi',1,'Which TWO actions belong to the "identify the problem" step of the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology? (Select TWO.)','["Inquire about recent environmental or infrastructure changes", "Gather information from the user and identify what they changed on the system", "Refer to the vendor''s instructions for guidance while carrying out the repair", "Conduct research based on the symptoms in order to form a hypothesis", "Implement preventive measures so the same problem does not recur"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'CompTIA lists both user-side information gathering and questions about environmental or infrastructure changes (a new access point, a firmware push, building work near a cable run, a power event) under step 1. Symptom research (option 4) sits in step 2, vendor instructions (option 3) in step 4, and preventive measures (option 5) in step 5 - each is a real troubleshooting action, just at a different point in the sequence.'),
('a1-q-809','aplus1',5,'5.1','multi',3,'A technician is about to implement a fix that requires restarting a departmental file server during business hours. Which TWO considerations does the CompTIA methodology require be weighed before implementing the change? (Select TWO.)','["Corporate policies and change-management procedures", "The impact the change will have on users and on the business", "Whether the user has been shown the six-step methodology", "Whether the replacement part comes from the same manufacturer as the original", "Whether the ticket has already been escalated to tier 2"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'The methodology carries a standing caveat: always consider corporate policies, procedures, and impact before implementing changes. Option 4 is the strongest distractor because part compatibility is a genuine engineering concern - but it is a technical detail of the plan, not the governance check CompTIA is testing. Escalation status and user education are not gating conditions for implementing an approved fix.'),
('a1-q-810','aplus1',5,'5.1','mcq',1,'A technician finishes repairing a workstation and writes the closure notes on the ticket. Which content best satisfies the final step of the methodology?','["The symptoms observed, the cause identified, the actions taken, and the outcome", "The date and time the ticket was opened and closed", "The user''s name, department, and asset tag", "A note stating that the issue is resolved and no further action is required"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Step 6 is "document the findings, actions, and outcomes" - all three, so that the next technician inherits the diagnosis rather than starting over. Option 4 is what many technicians actually write, which is what makes it tempting, but a bare "resolved" tells a colleague facing the same recurring symptom nothing about what was found or what was changed. Timestamps and asset details are ticket metadata, not findings.'),
('a1-q-811','aplus1',5,'5.1','mcq',2,'A user reports no network connectivity. The technician looks at the switch, sees the port link LED is dark, and says, "I think the patch cable has failed." Which step of the methodology has the technician just completed?','["Establish a theory of probable cause", "Test the theory to determine the cause", "Establish a plan of action to resolve the problem", "Verify full system functionality"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Naming a suspected cause is establishing a theory. Option 2 is the tempting pick because the technician did make an observation, but testing means performing an action that proves or disproves the theory - fitting a known-good patch cable and seeing whether the link LED comes up. On the exam, verbs like "suspects," "believes," or "thinks" signal step 2, while "swaps," "disconnects," or "tries to see if" signal step 3.'),
('a1-q-812','aplus1',5,'5.2','mcq',2,'A technician opens an unstable desktop and finds several cylindrical capacitors near the CPU socket with domed, split tops and a crusty brown residue. What is the correct course of action?','["Replace the motherboard - swollen capacitors mean the board''s power-regulation stage has failed", "Reapply thermal paste to the CPU and retest under load", "Reseat the memory modules and clear CMOS", "Replace the power supply and continue using the same motherboard"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Bulging or leaking electrolytic capacitors are a definitive sign that the board''s power filtering and regulation have failed, producing unstable rails, random reboots, and no-POST. Field repair means desoldering, so board replacement is the supported fix. Option 4 is the strong distractor: a failing PSU can indeed be what killed the capacitors and may also need replacing, but the damaged board will never be reliable again.'),
('a1-q-813','aplus1',5,'5.2','mcq',1,'A desktop shows no lights, no fan movement, and no beeps when the power button is pressed. The technician has already confirmed the wall outlet works with a known-good device. What should be checked NEXT?','["The PSU rocker switch and voltage selector, the mains lead, and the 24-pin ATX and EPS connectors at the board", "The boot order in UEFI setup", "The memory modules for correct dual-channel population", "The SATA data cable running to the boot drive"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'With zero signs of life the failure is upstream of POST, so it lies in the mains path: PSU switch and voltage selector, the power lead, the main 24-pin and CPU EPS connectors, or the front-panel power-button header. Option 3 is the reflex answer for many technicians, but bad or missing RAM still lets the fans spin and produces beeps or DRAM LEDs; boot order and SATA cabling only matter once the board is already powering up.'),
('a1-q-814','aplus1',5,'5.2','mcq',1,'A user reports a sharp burning-plastic and ozone smell coming from their desktop, which is still switched on and running. What should the technician do FIRST?','["Shut the system down and disconnect it from mains power", "Open Task Manager to find the process driving the load", "Run the built-in diagnostics from UEFI setup", "Raise the case fan speeds in UEFI to clear the smell"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A burning smell means a component - most often the PSU or a board power stage - is actively overheating or arcing, which is a fire and shock hazard. Safety comes before diagnosis: remove power, then inspect for scorch marks, melted connectors, and swollen capacitors. Option 4 is tempting because more airflow does address heat, but it leaves a failing component energized while you experiment.'),
('a1-q-815','aplus1',5,'5.2','multi',2,'A workstation shuts down abruptly with no blue screen after 20 to 40 minutes of heavy 3D work, then restarts normally once it has cooled. Which TWO causes are the most likely? (Select TWO.)','["A CPU cooler that has lost contact or has dried-out thermal paste, triggering thermal protection", "A power supply that cannot sustain its rated output under load and is tripping its protection circuit", "A corrupted Windows user profile", "An incorrect boot order in UEFI setup", "A missing printer driver"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'An instant power loss with no stop code is hardware protection acting, not software failing - which narrows it to thermal shutdown or a PSU over-current/over-temperature trip, both of which correlate with sustained load. A corrupted profile produces sign-in errors and application faults but cannot cut power, and boot order and drivers have no bearing on a machine that is already running.'),
('a1-q-816','aplus1',5,'5.2','mcq',3,'A desktop has developed a loud grinding noise whose pitch rises and falls with CPU load, and case temperatures have been climbing. What is the MOST likely source?','["A failing bearing in the CPU cooler fan", "A failing optical drive spindle motor", "A memory module that has worked loose in its slot", "Coil whine from the graphics card''s voltage regulators"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Only a mechanical bearing both grinds and tracks CPU load, and a fan losing RPM to a worn bearing also explains the rising temperatures - the two symptoms have one cause. Option 4 is the best distractor because coil whine genuinely does change with load, but it is a high-pitched electrical whine rather than a grind and it has no effect on cooling. An idle optical drive is not spinning, and RAM has no moving parts.'),
('a1-q-817','aplus1',5,'5.2','mcq',2,'A desktop loses its date and time and reverts its firmware settings to defaults every time it is unplugged overnight, and reports a checksum error at POST. What should the technician replace?','["The CMOS coin-cell battery on the motherboard (typically a CR2032)", "The power supply", "The motherboard, because the real-time clock is soldered to it", "The memory, because firmware settings are held in RAM"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'The coin cell supplies standby power to the real-time clock and the firmware settings store when mains power is removed; a depleted cell produces exactly this triad - wrong clock, defaults restored, CMOS checksum error. Option 3 is the tempting one because the RTC does live on the board, but the clock circuit itself is fine; it has simply lost its battery. A wrong system clock is worth fixing promptly, as it also breaks HTTPS certificate validation and domain logons.'),
('a1-q-818','aplus1',5,'5.2','matching',3,'Match each motherboard, RAM, CPU, or power symptom on the left to its most likely cause on the right.','{"left": ["The system powers on and fans spin, but there is no video and the board emits a repeating beep pattern", "Domed, leaking capacitors around the CPU socket, with random reboots", "The clock resets and firmware settings return to defaults after every disconnection from mains", "Abrupt power loss under combined CPU and GPU load, with no stop code and normal temperatures"], "right": ["A failing or undersized power supply tripping its protection circuit", "A depleted CMOS coin-cell battery", "A failed motherboard power-regulation stage", "Absent, unseated, or failed memory"]}'::jsonb,'[3, 2, 1, 0]'::jsonb,'POST runs before video initializes, so a beeping board with no picture is reporting a pre-video fault - most often memory. Swollen capacitors are a board-level power-delivery failure requiring board replacement. Settings lost across a power disconnection point at the coin cell, not the board. An abrupt load-triggered power loss with normal temperatures rules out thermal shutdown and points at the PSU''s protection circuit; the common error is calling this one "overheating" because the trigger is load.'),
('a1-q-819','aplus1',5,'5.2','mcq',2,'A user reports that one specific application crashes on launch, while every other program runs normally and the workstation has passed a full Windows Memory Diagnostic pass. What is the MOST likely cause?','["Corrupted application files or a missing or incompatible dependency for that program", "Failing RAM", "An overheating CPU", "A failing power supply"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A fault confined to one application, with clean memory tests and everything else stable, points at that application''s own files, runtime, or dependencies - repair or reinstall it. Option 2 is the reflex answer whenever the word "crash" appears, but memory faults are indiscriminate: they take down many unrelated programs, produce varying stop codes between reboots, and generally show up in a memory diagnostic. Overheating and PSU faults cause shutdowns and reboots, not a single reproducible application error.'),
('a1-q-820','aplus1',5,'5.2','multi',3,'A desktop powers on with fans and lights but never reaches POST and produces no video. Which THREE actions are appropriate early diagnostic steps? (Select THREE.)','["Reseat the memory, testing one known-good module in the board''s primary slot", "Read the board''s POST/debug LEDs or two-digit code display against the vendor''s table", "Disconnect drives and add-in cards to boot a minimal configuration", "Reinstall the operating system from recovery media", "Update the graphics driver from within Windows"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'POST happens before any operating system code runs, so options 4 and 5 are impossible as well as irrelevant - you cannot reach recovery media or a Windows desktop on a machine that never POSTs, which is exactly why they are attractive to someone who has not registered where the boot stops. Minimal-configuration booting, memory reseating, and reading the board''s stage LEDs each isolate the subsystem that is halting POST.'),
('a1-q-821','aplus1',5,'5.2','mcq',3,'A ticket records "Root cause: the PC keeps overheating." Why is this an inadequate root-cause statement?','["Overheating is a symptom; the cause is whatever prevents heat removal, such as a clogged heat sink, a seized fan, or degraded thermal paste", "Overheating is a cause rather than a symptom, so the wording is inverted", "The statement is acceptable as long as the outcome is also recorded", "The statement should have quoted the stop code the machine produced"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A root cause must name the condition that produced the symptom, because the fix and any preventive measure follow from the cause, not from the temperature reading. Option 3 is the tempting one - documenting the outcome is genuinely required by step 6 - but an outcome attached to an unnamed cause still leaves the next technician unable to tell whether the fan, the paste, the filter, or the case airflow was at fault. Thermal shutdowns typically produce no stop code at all.'),
('a1-q-822','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',2,'A workstation that worked yesterday now displays "No bootable device found" at power-on. UEFI setup lists the NVMe drive and reports it as healthy. What is the MOST likely cause?','["The boot order changed, or the EFI system partition / boot entry is damaged", "The NVMe drive has physically failed", "The system memory is faulty", "The video cable to the monitor is loose"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'If firmware can enumerate the drive and read its identity, the physical interface, power, and drive controller are all working - so the failure lies in what the firmware has been told to boot or in the boot files themselves. Check whether a USB or network entry was promoted above the drive, whether the mode switched between UEFI and legacy, and repair the EFI system partition. Option 2 is tempting because the phrase names the boot device, but a physically dead drive normally disappears from firmware setup entirely.'),
('a1-q-823','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',2,'A server''s hot-swap drive bay shows a steady amber status LED on one drive while the others show green activity. What does this indicate, and what should be done before acting?','["That member has faulted or been flagged as predicted-to-fail; confirm the slot in the RAID controller utility before removing anything", "That member is the array''s dedicated parity drive and is operating normally", "The chassis backplane firmware needs updating", "The array has just completed a rebuild and is healthy"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'On effectively every server backplane, green indicates power and activity while amber or red flags a failed or predicted-failure member. Confirming the slot in the controller utility matters because pulling the wrong drive out of an already-degraded array destroys it. Option 2 is designed to catch anyone who remembers RAID 4 - in RAID 5 and 6 parity is distributed across all members, so there is no dedicated parity drive to flag, and a completed rebuild returns the LED to green.'),
('a1-q-824','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',2,'A drive reports a S.M.A.R.T. status of "failure predicted," yet the operating system boots normally and no files are inaccessible. How should the technician interpret this?','["The drive''s own monitoring has crossed a failure threshold - back up and replace it while it still reads reliably", "S.M.A.R.T. reports only past errors, so no action is needed while the system still boots", "It is a firmware bug and should be cleared by resetting the S.M.A.R.T. counters", "The drive should be reformatted, which resets the failing attributes"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'S.M.A.R.T. attributes such as reallocated sector count, pending sector count, and spin-retry count are predictive - a threshold breach means the drive itself expects to fail, and the window while it is still readable is the cheapest time to migrate the data. Option 4 is the tempting one because a format can hide bad sectors from the file system, but the physical media damage and the underlying attribute counters remain unchanged.'),
('a1-q-825','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',3,'Users report that file copies to a departmental file server that used to finish in seconds now take minutes. The RAID 5 array reports "degraded" with one member offline. What best explains the slowdown?','["Every read touching the missing member must be reconstructed from parity, costing extra I/O across the surviving drives", "RAID 5 always performs worse than a single drive", "The server''s switch port has negotiated down to 10 Mbps half duplex", "The server needs more memory"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'In a degraded RAID 5, a single logical read of a stripe on the missing member becomes a read of every surviving block in that stripe plus an XOR to reconstruct the data, so throughput and IOPS drop sharply until the rebuild completes. Option 3 is a genuine cause of slow transfers and therefore the strongest distractor, but it would not make the controller report the array as degraded - the array status is the evidence already in front of you.'),
('a1-q-826','aplus1',5,'5.3','multi',3,'A four-drive RAID 5 array reports one failed member. Which TWO actions are appropriate? (Select TWO.)','["Verify that a current, restorable backup exists before starting any rebuild", "Fit a replacement drive of matching capacity and type so the array can rebuild", "Pull a second drive to test whether the array still functions", "Reinitialize the array to clear the error condition", "Convert the array to RAID 0 to recover the lost performance"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'RAID 5 tolerates exactly one lost member, so the array is currently running without redundancy. The two correct priorities are confirming a restorable backup - a rebuild is a long, heavy read of every surviving drive and is precisely when a second latent failure surfaces - and installing a matched replacement so redundancy is restored. Option 4 is the dangerous distractor: reinitializing does clear the error, by destroying the array''s data. RAID 0 has no redundancy whatsoever.'),
('a1-q-827','aplus1',5,'5.3','matching',3,'Match each storage symptom on the left to its most likely cause on the right.','{"left": ["Rhythmic clicking, after which the drive disappears from firmware setup", "\"No bootable device found\" although the drive is listed and healthy in UEFI setup", "A newly fitted second SSD is absent from File Explorer but shows in Disk Management as unallocated", "File-server copy speeds have fallen sharply and one array member is offline"], "right": ["The disk has not been initialized, partitioned, and formatted", "The RAID array is running degraded and reconstructing data from parity", "Head-actuator or spindle failure inside the mechanical hard drive", "A changed boot order or a damaged EFI system partition / boot entry"]}'::jsonb,'[2, 3, 0, 1]'::jsonb,'Clicking followed by the drive vanishing is mechanical - the heads cannot seek or park, so the drive stops responding to identification. A drive the firmware can see but not boot is a boot-configuration problem, not a hardware one. A new disk that Disk Management shows as unallocated is simply raw and needs initializing, partitioning, and formatting - it is not a fault at all. A degraded array reconstructing from parity explains a sudden throughput collapse without any single drive being slow.'),
('a1-q-828','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',1,'A technician fits a second SATA SSD. It appears in UEFI setup and in Windows Disk Management as "Unknown, Not Initialized," but never in File Explorer. What must be done?','["Initialize the disk (GPT or MBR), create a partition, and format it with a file system", "Replace the SATA data cable", "Update the motherboard chipset drivers", "Switch the storage controller from AHCI to RAID mode in UEFI"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Windows lists raw disks in Disk Management but cannot assign a drive letter until the disk carries a partition table and a formatted volume - this is the expected state of brand-new hardware, not a fault. Option 2 is the tempting hardware answer, but a cabling problem would keep the disk out of Disk Management and usually out of UEFI setup as well; the fact that both already see it rules the data path out.'),
('a1-q-829','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',3,'A database server''s storage shows low MB/s throughput, yet sequential benchmarks on the same drives look normal. The workload is many small random reads and writes. Which metric characterizes the bottleneck, and which upgrade addresses it?','["IOPS - move the workload to SSD or NVMe, which deliver orders of magnitude more random operations per second than mechanical drives", "Rotational latency - fit mechanical drives with a larger onboard cache buffer", "Sequential throughput - stripe more mechanical drives in RAID 0 to raise MB/s", "Seek time - schedule a weekly defragmentation of the array"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Small random I/O is measured in IOPS, not MB/s: a 7,200 RPM drive manages roughly 75-100 random IOPS and a 15,000 RPM SAS drive around 175-210, because every operation pays a seek and a rotational delay, whereas a SATA SSD delivers tens of thousands and NVMe far more. Option 3 is the strongest distractor because striping genuinely does raise sequential MB/s - but it barely improves random IOPS per spindle, which is the metric that is actually limiting this workload.'),
('a1-q-830','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',2,'A user reports that several documents now open with garbled content, and the Windows event log shows repeated bad-block entries for the system drive. What should the technician do FIRST?','["Copy or image the user''s data to another device immediately", "Run chkdsk /r to locate and remap the bad sectors, then keep using the drive", "Defragment the drive to consolidate the damaged areas", "Perform an in-place reinstall of Windows over the existing installation"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Bad-block events together with corrupted files mean the media is actively degrading, and every further hour of use risks more loss. Option 2 is the tempting one because chkdsk /r is the correct repair tool and is genuinely the right second step - but it reads and rewrites the entire surface, which is exactly the kind of sustained workload that finishes off a marginal drive. Get the data off first, then repair or replace.'),
('a1-q-831','aplus1',5,'5.3','multi',2,'A desktop''s mechanical hard drive is emitting repetitive clicking and occasional grinding, and boots now take several minutes. Which TWO statements are correct? (Select TWO.)','["The noise indicates mechanical failure of the head actuator or spindle assembly", "The priority is imaging or copying the data off the drive before anything else is attempted", "Running a full surface scan first is the safest way to protect the data", "This noise is normal for any drive more than three years old", "Reformatting the drive will silence the noise and restore reliability"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1]'::jsonb,'Clicking and grinding are physical sounds that only a drive with moving parts can make, and a healthy one should not - the heads are failing to seek or park, or the spindle bearing is going. Every powered minute risks total loss, so data rescue comes first. Option 3 is the plausible-sounding trap: a surface scan is precisely the prolonged head-movement workload most likely to kill the drive mid-scan, and reformatting changes nothing mechanical.'),
('a1-q-832','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',3,'After a failed member is replaced in a RAID 5 array, the controller begins a rebuild that will run for many hours. What is the primary risk during this window?','["A second drive failure during the rebuild would destroy the array, because RAID 5 tolerates only one lost member", "The array operates as RAID 0 until the rebuild completes", "Write caching is permanently disabled after any rebuild", "The array''s usable capacity is reduced by the rebuild"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'A rebuild reads every sector of every surviving member, which is both the heaviest load those similarly-aged drives have ever seen and a long stretch with no redundancy - which is why a verified backup and, where available, a hot spare matter so much, and why RAID 6 or RAID 10 is preferred for large arrays. Option 2 is the tempting mental model: the array has no redundancy during the rebuild, but it does not change RAID level, and usable capacity is fixed by the level and member count.'),
('a1-q-833','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',1,'A video editor built a two-drive RAID 0 array for scratch storage. One of the two drives fails. What is the outcome?','["All data on the array is lost, because RAID 0 stripes data across both members with no redundancy or parity", "The array runs degraded and can be rebuilt from the surviving drive", "Only the files that happened to be stored on the failed drive are lost", "The array automatically converts itself into a single-drive volume"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'RAID 0 splits every file into stripes written alternately across both members, so losing either one leaves the surviving stripes unusable - the only recovery is a restore from backup. Option 3 is the intuitive but wrong mental model: striping means no file is confined to a single drive. Only mirrored or parity levels (1, 5, 6, 10) can run degraded and rebuild.'),
('a1-q-834','aplus1',5,'5.3','multi',1,'An internal SATA hard drive that worked last week no longer appears in UEFI setup or in the operating system. Which THREE checks are appropriate? (Select THREE.)','["Confirm the SATA power connector from the PSU is fully seated on the drive", "Try a known-good SATA data cable and a different port on the motherboard", "Check that the SATA port has not been disabled in UEFI setup", "Assign the drive a new letter in Disk Management", "Rebuild the Windows Search index"]'::jsonb,'[0, 1, 2]'::jsonb,'A drive missing from firmware is invisible to the operating system by definition, so the fault lies in power, the data path, or the port configuration - and note that populating an M.2 slot often disables a shared SATA port automatically. Option 4 is the strong distractor because assigning a letter is a real fix for a drive that is present but unmounted; it presupposes the OS can already see the disk, which it cannot here. The search index has nothing to do with device detection.'),
('a1-q-835','aplus1',5,'5.3','mcq',3,'An SSD-based laptop that felt fast a year ago now stalls for long periods during large file writes, while read speeds remain acceptable. Which explanation should the technician investigate FIRST?','["The drive is nearly full and worn, leaving few free blocks for garbage collection and the fast write cache", "The drive needs defragmenting so that its files are stored contiguously", "Windows has disabled write caching on the volume by itself", "The drive requires a low-level format to restore write performance"]'::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,'Flash must be erased before it can be rewritten, so the controller depends on a pool of free blocks for garbage collection and its fast pseudo-SLC write cache. As the drive fills, that pool shrinks and writes fall back to slow read-modify-erase-write cycles - which matches the gradual, write-only decline described. Option 3 is the strongest distractor because disabled write caching genuinely does slow writes without affecting reads, but it does not switch itself off over a year of normal use. Defragmenting an SSD (option 2) gives no benefit and consumes program/erase cycles.');
insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, domain, objective, deck, front, back) values
('a1-f-800','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','What must a technician always consider before implementing a change, no matter how certain the diagnosis is?','Corporate policies, procedures, and the impact of the change. A confirmed root cause authorizes a plan of action, not an unscheduled outage - a production server swap normally still needs a change ticket and an approved maintenance window.'),
('a1-f-801','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','Where in the six-step methodology does "perform backups before making changes" belong?','Inside step 1, identify the problem - alongside gathering information from the user and identifying what the user changed. Backing up first protects the data in case the repair itself (a reimage, a firmware flash, a chkdsk on a dying drive) destroys it.'),
('a1-f-802','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','What does "question the obvious" mean in step 2 of the methodology?','When establishing a theory of probable cause, rule out the simplest explanations first: mains lead unplugged, PSU rocker off, wrong monitor input, Caps Lock, muted volume, disabled adapter. Obvious causes are both the most common and the cheapest to eliminate.'),
('a1-f-803','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','If testing the theory does NOT confirm the cause, what two options does the methodology give you?','Establish a new theory and test again, or escalate to someone with more expertise or authority. You never advance to a plan of action on an unproven theory - that is guessing with the customer''s hardware.'),
('a1-f-804','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','What does step 3 tell you to do once the theory IS confirmed?','Determine the next steps to resolve the problem - which means moving into step 4, establishing a plan of action and implementing the solution. Confirmation ends the diagnostic phase; it does not authorize an immediate unplanned repair.'),
('a1-f-805','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','Which step of the methodology tells you to refer to the vendor''s instructions for guidance?','Step 4, establish a plan of action to resolve the problem and implement the solution. Vendor documentation supplies the approved procedure, the correct firmware sequence, beep-code and POST-code tables, and the steps that keep the warranty intact.'),
('a1-f-806','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','Why is "the PC boots to the desktop" not enough to satisfy step 5?','Step 5 is verify FULL system functionality. Have the user exercise the applications, peripherals, printers, and network resources that were failing - a machine that POSTs and boots can still carry the original fault, and the user is the only one who knows what "working" looks like.'),
('a1-f-807','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','What is the "if applicable" clause attached to step 5?','Implement preventive measures. After a thermal shutdown, schedule dust cleaning; after a drive failure, add S.M.A.R.T. monitoring or a hot spare; after a power event, add a UPS. Verification proves the fix worked; prevention stops the ticket coming back.'),
('a1-f-808','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','What three things must step 6 documentation capture?','Findings (the symptoms observed and the cause identified), actions (what was actually changed or replaced), and outcomes (whether it worked). Writing only "resolved" leaves the next technician with nothing when the same symptom returns in six weeks.'),
('a1-f-809','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','In an exam scenario, how do you tell "establish a theory" apart from "test the theory"?','Establishing a theory is stating a suspected cause - "I think the cable is bad." Testing is an action that proves or disproves it - fitting a known-good cable and watching the link LED. Verbs like suspects, believes, thinks signal step 2; swaps, disables, disconnects, boots from signal step 3.'),
('a1-f-810','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','Besides what the user changed, what else does step 1 tell you to ask about?','Environmental and infrastructure changes: a new access point or switch, a firmware or policy push, a building power event, construction near a cable run, a desk move, a new HVAC unit or space heater. Many faults correlate with a change nobody thought to mention.'),
('a1-f-811','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','At which step does the methodology permit external or internal research, and what does that look like?','Step 2, establish a theory of probable cause - "if necessary, conduct external or internal research based on symptoms." In practice: vendor knowledge bases, beep-code and POST-code tables, stop-code lookups, and your own ticket history for the same asset or symptom.'),
('a1-f-812','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','The root cause is confirmed and the spare part is in your hand. Why might you still not fix it right now?','Because step 4 requires weighing corporate policy, procedure, and impact first. On a production system that usually means a change ticket, an approved maintenance window, a communication to affected users, and a rollback plan - certainty about the cause does not exempt you from change control.'),
('a1-f-813','aplus1',5,'5.1','core','What is the single most common trap in Domain 5.1 "what should the technician do NEXT" questions?','Every distractor is a legitimate troubleshooting action placed at the wrong point in the sequence. Work out where the scenario already sits in the six steps, then choose the step that immediately follows - not the action that sounds most useful or most thorough.'),
('a1-f-814','aplus1',5,'5.2','acronym','POST','Power-On Self-Test. The firmware routine that checks the CPU, memory, and core chipset before handing control to a boot device. Because it runs before video is initialized, it reports failures through beep codes, POST/debug LEDs, or a two-digit code display.'),
('a1-f-815','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','What do swollen or leaking capacitors on a motherboard tell you about the repair plan?','The board''s power filtering and regulation stage has failed. Domed tops, split vent scores, or crusty brown/orange residue mean replace the motherboard - capacitor replacement is a soldering-bench job, not a field fix, and the board stays unstable until it is done. Check the PSU too, since a bad one may have caused the damage.'),
('a1-f-816','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','A PC shows no lights, no fans, and no beeps at all. What is the checklist?','Test the wall outlet with a known-good device, then the mains lead, the PSU rocker switch and voltage selector, the 24-pin ATX and the 4/8-pin EPS CPU connectors, and the front-panel power-button header (briefly jump the PWR_SW pins to bypass the case button). No POST activity means the fault is before POST, so boot order and OS settings are irrelevant.'),
('a1-f-817','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','What is the first action when a running computer smells of burning plastic or ozone?','Shut it down and unplug it immediately. An actively failing PSU or board power stage is a fire and shock hazard. Only then inspect for scorch marks, melted or discoloured connectors, and swollen capacitors. Never leave the system energized to "see what happens."'),
('a1-f-818','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','How do you tell a thermal shutdown apart from a power-supply shutdown?','Both cut power instantly with no blue screen. A thermal shutdown builds up - fans ramp, the CPU throttles, temperatures climb, and it recurs at a predictable point under sustained load. A PSU protection trip fires when a rail is overloaded, often at a GPU power spike, and can happen with entirely normal temperatures. Log temperatures under load to separate them.'),
('a1-f-819','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','What causes a PC''s clock and firmware settings to reset every time it is unplugged?','A depleted CMOS coin cell, usually a CR2032. It keeps the real-time clock and settings store alive without mains power. Symptoms: wrong date/time, "CMOS checksum error" at POST, boot order back to defaults - and downstream failures such as HTTPS certificate errors and Kerberos/domain logon problems caused by the wrong clock.'),
('a1-f-820','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','How do you tell whether random crashes come from failing RAM or from software?','Memory faults are indiscriminate: many unrelated applications crash, blue-screen stop codes differ between reboots, and MemTest86 or Windows Memory Diagnostic reports errors. A single application crashing while everything else is stable and memory tests clean points to that program''s own files or dependencies.'),
('a1-f-821','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','What does a repeating POST beep pattern with no video usually indicate, and how do you act on it?','A fault detected before video initialization - most commonly absent, unseated, or failed memory, with the graphics card the next most likely. Reseat one known-good module in the board''s primary slot, then check the pattern against that vendor''s beep-code table. Beep codes are vendor-specific, so never read them off a generic chart.'),
('a1-f-822','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','What is a minimal-configuration boot and when do you use it?','When a system will not POST: strip it to motherboard, CPU with cooler, one memory module, and PSU - plus a graphics card only if there is no integrated video - and disconnect drives, add-in cards, and front-panel headers. If it POSTs, reintroduce one component at a time until the fault returns; that names the failing part.'),
('a1-f-823','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','Why is "the PC is overheating" not a root cause?','It is a symptom. The root cause is whatever blocks heat removal: a dust-clogged heat sink or intake filter, a seized or unplugged fan, dried or pumped-out thermal paste, a cooler not seated flat, or a case with no intake path. Document the cause rather than the temperature, so a preventive measure can actually be chosen.'),
('a1-f-824','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','What is the most likely source of a grinding noise inside a desktop?','A mechanical part - a fan bearing in the CPU cooler, a case fan, or the PSU, or a mechanical drive or optical drive. Noise whose pitch tracks CPU load usually means the CPU cooler fan, and a fan losing RPM also explains rising temperatures. Coil whine from a GPU or PSU also varies with load, but it is a high-pitched electrical whine and does not affect cooling.'),
('a1-f-825','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','How do POST/debug LEDs and two-digit POST code displays help you troubleshoot a no-boot?','Most modern boards light a stage LED (CPU, DRAM, VGA, BOOT) or display a hex code at the point where POST halts, naming the subsystem that failed. Read the indicator against the board manual before disassembling anything - it converts guesswork into a targeted reseat, swap, or minimal-config test.'),
('a1-f-826','aplus1',5,'5.2','core','Which motherboard, CPU, and RAM faults show up as sluggish performance rather than a crash?','Thermal throttling from a failing cooler or clogged heat sink, insufficient RAM causing constant paging to disk, memory installed in single channel or with mismatched modules so it runs below its rated speed, and firmware power settings capping CPU multipliers. Check temperatures, memory utilization, and the reported memory speed and channel count before blaming software.'),
('a1-f-827','aplus1',5,'5.3','acronym','S.M.A.R.T.','Self-Monitoring, Analysis, and Reporting Technology. Firmware inside a drive that tracks health attributes such as reallocated sectors, pending sectors, spin-retry count, and SSD wear levelling, and raises a predicted-failure warning so data can be migrated before the drive dies.'),
('a1-f-828','aplus1',5,'5.3','acronym','RAID','Redundant Array of Independent Disks. Combines multiple drives into one logical volume for performance (striping), fault tolerance (mirroring or parity), or both. It protects against drive hardware failure only - never against deletion, ransomware, or corruption - so it is not a backup.'),
('a1-f-829','aplus1',5,'5.3','acronym','IOPS','Input/Output Operations Per Second. The count of discrete read/write operations storage can service each second, and the meaningful metric for small random workloads such as databases and virtual-machine hosts, where MB/s throughput hides the real bottleneck.'),
('a1-f-830','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','What do drive-bay status LEDs mean on a server backplane, and what must you check before pulling a drive?','Green normally means power and activity, blinking green means I/O or an in-progress rebuild, and amber or red flags a failed or predicted-failure member. Always confirm which physical slot the RAID controller is reporting before removing anything - pulling the wrong member from an already-degraded array destroys it.'),
('a1-f-831','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','What do clicking and grinding noises from a drive mean, and what is the first action?','They are mechanical: a failing head actuator (the "click of death"), a failing spindle bearing, or head-to-platter contact. Only a hard disk can make them; an SSD is silent. Stop using the drive and image or copy the data off first - surface scans, chkdsk /r, and defragmentation all add hours of head movement to a dying mechanism.'),
('a1-f-832','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','Firmware can see the drive but the PC reports "no bootable device found." What does that narrow the fault to?','The physical path and drive electronics are fine, so suspect boot configuration: a changed boot order (a USB or PXE entry promoted above the drive), a UEFI-versus-legacy or Secure Boot mismatch, or a deleted or corrupted EFI system partition or boot record. Repair the boot entry rather than replacing hardware.'),
('a1-f-833','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','A brand-new disk shows in Disk Management but has no drive letter. What is missing?','Initialization (GPT or MBR), a partition, and a file-system format. A raw disk is the normal state of new hardware, not a fault. If the disk is missing from Disk Management as well, go back a layer to power, data cable, and whether the SATA or M.2 port is enabled in firmware.'),
('a1-f-834','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','What does a S.M.A.R.T. "failure predicted" warning oblige you to do?','Treat it as a countdown: back up or image immediately, then replace the drive. It is still readable now, which is the cheapest moment to migrate. Do not clear the warning, reformat, or suppress the alert in firmware - the underlying reallocated and pending sector damage is physical and remains.'),
('a1-f-835','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','Why does a degraded RAID 5 array run so much slower than a healthy one?','Every read that touches the missing member must be reconstructed: the controller reads the corresponding blocks from all surviving drives and XORs them, turning one logical read into several physical ones. Throughput and IOPS fall sharply until the rebuild finishes, and there is no redundancy left in the meantime.'),
('a1-f-836','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','What happens when a single drive fails in RAID 0, 1, 5, 6, and 10?','RAID 0: everything is lost, there is no redundancy. RAID 1: keeps running on the mirror. RAID 5: runs degraded, tolerating one failed member. RAID 6: tolerates two failed members. RAID 10: survives one failure per mirrored pair. Only RAID 0 loses all data to a single drive failure.'),
('a1-f-837','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','What is the main risk during a RAID rebuild, and how do you reduce it?','The rebuild reads every sector of every surviving member for hours, with no redundancy in place - exactly the conditions that expose a second latent failure and lose the array. Verify a restorable backup before starting, keep a hot spare so rebuilds begin automatically, and prefer RAID 6 or RAID 10 for large arrays.'),
('a1-f-838','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','What are the likely causes when a drive is missing from the operating system entirely?','An unseated or loose SATA power or data connector, a failed cable, a SATA or M.2 port disabled in firmware (M.2 slots often share lanes and silently disable a SATA port), a raw uninitialized disk, or a genuinely dead drive. Check whether firmware setup sees it first - that single test splits hardware faults from OS-level ones.'),
('a1-f-839','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','What causes extended read and write times on a mechanical drive?','Bad sectors forcing retries and reallocation, a nearly full or heavily fragmented volume, a degraded RAID array reconstructing from parity, a failing SATA cable causing link resets, or heads re-seeking on a dying drive. Check S.M.A.R.T. attributes and the event log for bad-block and disk-controller-reset entries before concluding the drive is merely slow.'),
('a1-f-840','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','Why does an SSD''s write performance fall as the drive fills up?','Flash must be erased before it can be rewritten, and the controller needs free blocks for garbage collection and for its fast pseudo-SLC write cache. A nearly full drive, or one where TRIM is not running, exhausts that pool and falls back to slow read-modify-erase-write cycles. Free space and TRIM fix it; defragmenting does not and just burns program/erase cycles.'),
('a1-f-841','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','Why is RAID not a backup?','RAID protects against drive hardware failure and nothing else. Deleted files, ransomware encryption, file corruption, a failed controller, an accidental array reinitialization, fire, and theft all propagate to every member instantly. Redundancy keeps a service online; only backups let you recover a previous state.'),
('a1-f-842','aplus1',5,'5.3','core','Where do you look for evidence of storage-level data corruption before blaming the application?','Windows Event Viewer under the disk and Ntfs sources for bad-block and controller-reset events, the drive''s S.M.A.R.T. attributes (reallocated and pending sector counts), chkdsk output, and the RAID controller''s own event log. Several unrelated files corrupting at once points at the media or controller rather than one broken program.');
